  "prompt": "Feature: AI recipe import uses every source it is given, translates in its own step, and reports translated errors.\n\nThe AI import page posts to POST /api/recipes/create/ai, or its SSE twin /api/recipes/create/ai/stream, a multipart form with content, url, images, translateLanguage and createNewOrganizers. Three problems: pasted content replaces the other sources instead of adding to them (with a URL the page is not read at all; with images the text only rides along the image reading); translation is a clause prepended to the build request, which changes what the build step asks for and can make the provider invent a recipe; and the failure message the page shows as-is is often an exception's own text or class name.\n\nExpected:\n\n1. Every source is compiled and combined. Images, the URL and the pasted content are each compiled on their own, by today's compilers, and merged into the single compiled source document that the build, translation and organizer steps read. The URL is downloaded as a video when it is one and an audio provider is set, otherwise fetched once as a page through the safe_scrape_html imported by the module that defines CompileSourceStep. Pasted content is compiled as when it is the only source (plain text, schema.org JSON and HTML with ld+json need no provider call). Images plus content: one compile request for the images (response schema OpenAICompiledSource) then the build request (OpenAIRecipe), whose message contains both the text compiled from the images and the pasted content. URL plus content: the build message contains what the page says (the name in its ld+json) and the pasted content; orgURL is the URL. Video URL plus content: the build message contains the transcript and the pasted content, and the URL is never fetched as a page. A video URL without an audio provider is fetched as a page, once.\n2. The merged document: a single usable source is used as is; several usable sources stay distinguishable, the user's pasted content last and authoritative where they disagree; their combined length is capped with truncate_source_parts (item 6) so a long page cannot crowd out pasted text; the document keeps the first language and the first image URL the usable sources report (uploaded images still take precedence as the recipe image). A source is usable when it contains a recipe and its content is not blank. Sources compiled but none usable: 400 with recipe.import-errors.no-recipe-found; nothing could be compiled: unreadable-source; an empty request: no-source.\n3. HTML the URL and html-or-json importers already hold (their AI fallback strategy, RecipeScraperOpenAI) is that URL's own page, not extra content: the workflow must not fetch the URL again nor compile the page twice. html-or-json with data and url answers 201 without fetching, with the provider's name and orgURL the url. URL import via AI and html-or-json without url still give 201 with the provider's name, ingredients and instructions; an organizer request is made only when include_tags or include_categories asks for it, tags are imported title-cased; the html-or-json stream emits progress and done events; 400 when the provider returns nothing or no default AI provider is set.\n4. Translation is its own step. The build request is identical with or without translateLanguage and never mentions the language. After the recipe is built, and before organizers are resolved, a separate provider request (response schema OpenAIRecipe, a new stored system prompt loaded with get_prompt, kept with the recipe prompts) translates the built recipe. Its message contains the target language exactly as sent (\"French\") and the whole built recipe in the provider's recipe schema: name, description, yield and times, each ingredient's text and section title, each instruction's text and title, each note's text and title. The answer replaces the draft recipe, converted exactly as the build answer is (section titles, notes, image, orgURL, owner), keeps the untranslated recipe's nutrition, and is cleaned again; the saved recipe has the translated name. With translation the provider gets exactly two OpenAIRecipe requests, build then translate. No translation request when no language is asked for, or when the compiled source's language equals the requested one ignoring case (\"French\" source, \"french\" requested). The step declares the progress key recipe.create-progress.translating-recipe, and DEFAULT_WORKFLOW_STEPS now runs compile, build, translate, organizers.\n5. The error message (detail.message of the 400, and the stream's error event message) is always a translated string in the request's language and never contains the exception's text or class name: RateLimitError gives the existing exceptions.rate-limit-error text; NoRecipeDataError and AIProviderNotEnabledError their own already-translated message; OpenAINotEnabledException (exported from the openai services package) recipe.import-errors.ai-not-enabled; VideoDownloadError recipe.import-errors.video-download-failed (new); OpenAIServiceError recipe.import-errors.ai-request-failed (new); scraper HTTPExceptions keep reporting their detail (the ParserErrors value for the URL and HTML importers); anything else, for example RuntimeError(\"psycopg2.OperationalError: ...\"), recipe.import-errors.unknown-error (new).\n6. truncate_source_parts(parts, max_length=MAX_SOURCE_CONTENT_LENGTH) -> list[str], next to truncate_source_content in the module holding MAX_SOURCE_CONTENT_LENGTH and TRUNCATION_NOTICE: caps the texts so their combined length fits max_length, in input order; every part gets an equal integer share, a shorter part keeps its text and leaves its unused share to the longer ones, and a part over its final allowance is cut like truncate_source_content (first allowance characters plus the notice, not counted). [\"a\"*10, \"b\"*10] with 100 is unchanged; [\"a\"*100, \"b\"*100] with 10 gives [\"a\"*5 + notice, \"b\"*5 + notice]; [\"a\"*1000, \"b\"*10] with 100 gives [\"a\"*90 + notice, \"b\"*10]; [] gives []. truncate_source_content itself does not change.\n7. en-US backend messages: add recipe.import-errors.ai-request-failed, unknown-error and video-download-failed and recipe.create-progress.translating-recipe (\"Translating recipe...\"), each with a short user-facing text. Every key under recipe.import-errors and recipe.create-progress is referenced in backend Python as a complete quoted literal and every such literal resolves to an en-US text; nothing is orphaned. The referenced import-error keys are then ai-not-enabled, ai-request-failed, image-provider-not-enabled, no-recipe-found, no-source, provider-returned-nothing, unknown-error, unreadable-source and video-download-failed; the progress keys declared by DEFAULT_WORKFLOW_STEPS and DEFAULT_SOURCE_COMPILERS stay as they are plus translating-recipe, each once.\n8. The existing POST /api/recipes/create/ai behaviours keep working: content-only text and schema.org JSON make no compile request; images get a compile then a build request and the first image is saved as original.webp; a URL whose page carries ld+json gives 201 with orgURL set; a video URL with an audio provider is transcribed, never fetched; organizers (none requested when the group has none and createNewOrganizers is off; existing ones attached and unknown names dropped; new ones created when createNewOrganizers is \"true\"; the organizer request reads the compiled source, so a \"tags: ...\" line in pasted text reaches it; the group's tags are listed in the organizer prompt; an organizer failure does not lose the recipe); the no-image placeholder is never downloaded or stored; RateLimitError gives 400 mentioning \"rate limiting\"; section titles and nutrition survive (\"For the sauce\" and null, \"Prep\" and \"\", calories \"250\", proteinContent \"12\" from \"12 g\"); 400 for no source, a source without a recipe, a provider returning nothing, no default AI provider, images without an image provider; the stream answers 200 with progress and done events or ends with an error event.\n\nThe UI hint changes on the AI import page are not part of this task.\n\nTests: `python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider tests/integration_tests/user_recipe_tests tests/unit_tests/services_tests` for quick loops; the whole backend suite is `python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider tests` (about 8 minutes).\n",
